From: microvm <microvm@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/ibt: don't seal ENDBRs on CPUs without IBT

apply_seal_endbr() overwrites the ENDBR at the entry of every function
whose address is never taken, so IBT faults on an indirect call to it. On
a CPU without IBT, ENDBR and the poison are both NOPs and the rewrite has
no effect, other than costing a text write per function at boot and
dirtying most kernel text pages, which then can't stay shared between VMs
that map the same kernel image. Skip it there. IBT is decided once at
boot, so the ENDBRs can't need sealing later.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c	2026-11-04 08:50:04.517998377 +0100
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c	2026-11-04 08:50:04.562802626 +0100
@@ -1244,6 +1244,14 @@
 {
 	s32 *s;
 
+	/*
+	 * Without IBT, ENDBR and its poison are both NOPs: sealing would only
+	 * rewrite (and dirty) the entry of every function never called
+	 * indirectly. IBT can't be turned on later.
+	 */
+	if (!cpu_feature_enabled(X86_FEATURE_IBT))
+		return;
+
 	for (s = start; s < end; s++) {
 		void *addr = (void *)s + *s;
 
